%I A047085 #38 Aug 05 2026 22:27:11
%S A047085 1,1,3,9,27,83,259,817,2599,8323,26797,86659,281287,915907,2990383,
%T A047085 9786369,32092959,105435607,346950321,1143342603,3772698725,
%U A047085 12463525229,41218894577,136451431723,452116980643,1499282161375,4975631425581,16524213199923,54913514061867
%N A047085 a(n) = T(2*n, n), array T as in A047080.
%C A047085 This is the number of walks from (0,0) to (n,n) where unit horizontal (+1,0), vertical (0,+1), and diagonal (+1,+1) steps are permitted but a horizontal step cannot be followed by a vertical step, nor vice versa.
%C A047085 a(n) is also the number of walks from (0,0) to (n,n) with steps that increment one or two coordinates and having the property that no two consecutive steps are orthogonal. - _Lee A. Newberg_, Dec 04 2009
%C A047085 a(n) is also the number of standard sequence alignments of two strings of length n, counting only those alignments with the property that, for every pair of consecutive alignment columns, there is at least one sequence that contributes a non-gap to both columns. That is, a(n) counts only those standard alignments with a column order that can be unambiguously reconstructed from the knowledge of all pairings, where a pairing is, e.g., that some i-th position of the first string is in the same column as some j-th position of the second string. - _Lee A. Newberg_, Dec 11 2009
%C A047085 First differences of A108626: a(n) = A108626(n) - A108626(n-1) for n>=1. - _Thomas Baruchel_, Nov 08 2014
%C A047085 The number of walls of height one in all bargraphs of semiperimeter n>=2. A wall is a maximal sequence of adjacent up steps. - _Arnold Knopfmacher_, Nov 04 2016
%C A047085 Main diagonal of Table 2 in Covington. - _Peter Bala_, Jan 27 2018
%C A047085 From _Thierry Marchant_, Oct 30 2024: (Start)
%C A047085 a(n) is also the number of maximal antichains in the product of two chains of length n.
%C A047085 a(n) is also the number of strict chains in the product of two chains of length n (a strict chain P in a product of two chains is a chain such that x,y in P implies x_1 different from y_1 and x_2 different from y_2). (End)
%C A047085 a(n) is the number of simple protocols in a game where each player has n moves. A simple protocol is a sequence of A's, B's, and AB's indicating a move by Alice, a move by Bob, or simultaneous moves by both. - _Steven E Landsburg_, Jul 21 2026
%H A047085 G. C. Greubel, <a href="/A047085/b047085.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%H A047085 Aubrey Blecher, Charlotte Brennan, and Arnold Knopfmacher, <a href="https://doi.org/10.61091/ojac-1206">Walls in bargraphs</a>, Online J. Anal. Comb., Issue 12 (2017), 1-11 (Paper #6).
%H A047085 Denis Bouyssou, Thierry Marchant, and Marc Pirlot, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16243">About maximal antichains in a product of two chains:A catch-all note</a>, arXiv:2410.16243 [math.CO], 2024. See pp. 1, 3, 16-18. [References A171155, a duplicate of this sequence.]
%H A047085 Denis Bouyssou, Thierry Marchant, and Marc Pirlot, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18443">ELECTRE TRI-nB, pseudo-disjunctive: axiomatic and combinatorial results</a>, arXiv:2410.18443 [cs.DM], 2024. See p. 14. [References A171155.]
%H A047085 Michael A. Covington, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/0929617042000314921">The Number of Distinct Alignments of Two Strings</a>, J. Quantit. Linguistics 11(3) (2004), 173-182. [References A171155.]
%H A047085 Steven E. Landsburg, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.17102">Some Integer Sequences Arising from Game Theory</a>, arXiv:2607.17102 [math.CO], 2026. See p. 5. [References A171155.]
%F A047085 D-finite with recurrence a(n) = ((4*n-3)*a(n-1)-(2*n-5)*a(n-2)+a(n-3)-(n-3)*a(n-4))/n. - _Alois P. Heinz_, Jan 22 2013
%F A047085 G.f.: sqrt((1 - x)/(1 - 3*x - x^2 - x^3)). - _Mark van Hoeij_, May 10 2013
%F A047085 G.f.: Sum_{n>=0} (2*n)!/n!^2 * x^(2*n) / (1-2*x)^(3*n+1). - _Paul D. Hanna_, Sep 21 2013
%F A047085 G.f.: Sum_{n>=0} x^n/(1-x)^n * Sum_{k=0..n} C(n,k)^2 * x^k. - _Paul D. Hanna_, Nov 08 2014
%e A047085 For n = 3, the 9 alignments are:
%e A047085   ABC A-BC ABC- -ABC -ABC --ABC ABC- AB-C ABC--
%e A047085   DEF DEF- D-EF DEF- DE-F DEF-- -DEF -DEF --DEF
%p A047085 a:= proc(n) option remember; `if`(n<4, [1, 1, 3, 9][n+1],
%p A047085       ((4*n-3)*a(n-1) -(2*n-5)*a(n-2) +a(n-3) -(n-3)*a(n-4))/n)
%p A047085     end:
%p A047085 seq(a(n), n=0..30);  # _Alois P. Heinz_, Jan 22 2013
%t A047085 CoefficientList[Series[Sqrt[(1-x)/(1-3*x-x^2-x^3)], {x, 0, 50}], x] (* _Vincenzo Librandi_, Nov 09 2014 *) (* _G. C. Greubel_, Oct 30 2022 *)
%o A047085 (Magma) R<x>:=PowerSeriesRing(Rationals(), 50); Coefficients(R!( Sqrt((1-x)/(1 -3*x-x^2-x^3)) )); // _G. C. Greubel_, Oct 30 2022
%o A047085 (PARI) my(x='x+O('x^30)); Vec(sqrt((1-x)/(1-3*x-x^2-x^3))) \\ _Joerg Arndt_, May 11 2013
%o A047085 (PARI) {a(n)=polcoeff(sum(m=0, n, (2*m)!/m!^2 * x^(2*m) / (1-x+x*O(x^n))^(3*m+1)), n)} \\ _Paul D. Hanna_, Sep 21 2013
%o A047085 (PARI) {a(n)=polcoeff( sum(m=0,n, x^m * sum(k=0,m, binomial(m,k)^2 * x^k) / (1-x +x*O(x^n))^m) ,n)}
%o A047085 for(n=0,30,print1(a(n),", ")) \\ _Paul D. Hanna_, Nov 08 2014
%o A047085 (PARI) a(n)=sum(k=0, n, sum(i=0, k, binomial(n-k, i)^2*binomial(n-i, k-i)))-sum(k=0, n-1, sum(i=0, k, binomial(n-k-1, i)^2*binomial(n-i-1, k-i))) \\ _Thomas Baruchel_, Nov 09 2014
%o A047085 (SageMath)
%o A047085 def A047085_list(prec):
%o A047085     P.<x> = PowerSeriesRing(ZZ, prec)
%o A047085     return P( sqrt((1-x)/(1-3*x-x^2-x^3)) ).list()
%o A047085 A047085_list(50) # _G. C. Greubel_, Oct 30 2022
%Y A047085 Cf. A047080, A047081, A047082, A047083, A047084, A047086, A047087, A047088.
%Y A047085 See A171158 for the number of such walks in three dimensions. - _Lee A. Newberg_, Dec 04 2009
%Y A047085 See A171563 for the number of such walks in four dimensions. - _Lee A. Newberg_, Dec 11 2009
